section.para:
			This example uses a 45-byte PNG header fixture with dimensions 5 by 7:

String text = "An image data:image/png;base64,"
    + "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUAAAAHCAYAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"
    + " follows.";

AssetDetector detector = new CursorAssetDetector();
EmbeddedAsset asset = detector.detect(text).get(0);
// asset.format()        = "png"
// asset.mediaType()     = "image/png"
// asset.width()         = 5
// asset.height()        = 7
// asset.decodedLength() = 45
byte[] bytes = asset.decode(text);  // the original 45 bytes

			The asset span covers the URI and the payload span covers the base64 text.
			decode returns the payload bytes from the original text.

			Detection needs no data: URI: the same payload pasted bare into
			prose is found by its magic prefix and verified against its decoded header:

String bare = "A report pastes "
    + "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAUAAAAHCAYAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"
    + " into the body.";

EmbeddedAsset found = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(bare).get(0);
// found.format()        = "png", sniffed from the decoded header
// found.mediaType()     = "image/png", derived from the format
// found.width()         = 5
// found.height()        = 7
// found.decodedLength() = 45

			For a bare run, the asset span and payload span cover the same characters.

			A short text payload does not require a file signature:

String note = "data:text/plain;charset=UTF-8;base64,SGk=";
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(note).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(note), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
// decoded = "Hi"
// asset.format() = "plain"
// asset.mediaType() = "text/plain;charset=UTF-8"

			An MPX attachment can identify a program other than Microsoft Project. This
			example contains an original file-creation record and a comment:

String project = "MPX;Fieldwork Planner;4.0;ANSI\r\n0;Survey schedule\r\n";
String text = "data:;base64," + Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(
    project.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(text).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(text), StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);
// asset.format() = "mpx"
// asset.mediaType() = "application/x-project"
// decoded equals project

			A calendar attachment retains its original text, including letter case:

String calendar = "begin:vcalendar\r\n"
    + "PRODID:-//Fieldwork Planner//EN\r\n"
    + "VERSION:2.0\r\nBEGIN:VEVENT\r\nUID:survey@example.org\r\n"
    + "DTSTAMP:20260907T090000Z\r\nDTSTART:20260908T100000Z\r\n"
    + "SUMMARY:Survey schedule\r\nEND:VEVENT\r\nEND:VCALENDAR\r\n";
String text = "data:;base64," + Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(
    calendar.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(text).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(text), StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);
// asset.format() = "ics"
// asset.mediaType() = "text/calendar"
// decoded equals calendar

			This SVG attachment contains an original circle image:

String svg = "<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 10 10'>"
    + "<circle cx='5' cy='5' r='4'/></svg>";
String text = "data:;base64," + Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(
    svg.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(text).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(text), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
// asset.format() = "svg"
// asset.mediaType() = "image/svg+xml"
// decoded equals svg

			A caption attachment without a supplied media type is identified by its header:

String captions = "\uFEFFWEBVTT\tTrail captions\n\n"
    + "00:00.000 --> 00:03.000\nTurn left at the bridge.\n";
String text = "data:;base64,"
    + Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(captions.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(text).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(text), StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
// asset.format() = "vtt"
// asset.mediaType() = "text/vtt"
// decoded equals captions, including the byte-order mark

			PEM text can also appear inside base64. This example uses public elliptic-curve
			parameters for prime256v1, not a private key:

String parameters = "-----BEGIN EC PARAMETERS-----\n"
    + "BggqhkjOPQMBBw==\n"
    + "-----END EC PARAMETERS-----\n";
String encoded = Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(
    parameters.getBytes(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));
EmbeddedAsset asset = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(encoded).get(0);
String decoded = new String(asset.decode(encoded), StandardCharsets.US_ASCII);
// asset.format() = "pem-parameters"
// asset.mediaType() = "application/x-x509-ec-parameters"
// decoded equals parameters

			The media type distinguishes EC parameters from DSA parameters. Decoding the
			outer base64 returns the PEM text. Encoded PEM detection requires a complete
			supported opening
			marker at byte 0, including the label and its five closing hyphens.
			It does not validate the end marker, body or cryptographic data.

			Compact JSON Web Tokens from
			RFC 7519
			are recognized as three base64url segments. The header and claims must decode
			as UTF-8 JSON objects, the header must carry a non-empty string-valued
			alg, and each segment is bounded to 16 KiB. For an unsecured token,
			alg must be none and the signature segment must be empty;
			all other algorithms require a non-empty signature segment with valid unpadded
			base64url encoding. The full token is
			the asset span and the claims segment is the payload:

String jwt = "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9"
    + ".eyJzdWIiOiIxMjMifQ.AAAA";

EmbeddedAsset token = new CursorAssetDetector().detect(jwt).get(0);
// token.format()    = "jwt"
// token.mediaType() = "application/jwt"
// new String(token.decode(jwt), StandardCharsets.UTF_8) = "{\"sub\":\"123\"}"

			Detection validates structure only. It does not verify a JWT signature, trust a
			key, or make an authorization decision.
